vaevictis-tools (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * VPN helper: every value of the copy that WireGuard reads is written again
    in its standard form, never copied from the file; IPv6 scope IDs ("%")
    are refused (1.3 copied an Address like "fe80::1%..." as it was: not
    run by wg-quick, and only with IPv6 switched back on, but the copy was
    meant to hold checked values only); ListenPort is left out (a client
    does not listen); a second check, independent of the parser, reads the
    finished copy and starts nothing if anything unexpected is in it.
  * VPN manager: the VaeVictis colours (violet, lilac, red only for
    disconnecting and errors) and a dark title bar.
  * For systems installed from 1.1 or 1.2: copies from vaevictis-tools 1.3.0
    (the same as the 1.2 image) are recognised and updated.

 -- linuxtuts-website <292920559+linuxtuts-website@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 21 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200

vaevictis-tools (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Now also carries VaeVictis PDF and LinPaint+. Fixes found with an
    AI-assisted review of the apps (Argon by Google; not an independent
    security audit):
  * VaeVictis PDF: adding pages from a password-protected PDF asks whether
    to protect the whole document with that password (they were saved
    unprotected, without a word); a PDF with only permission restrictions
    says that saving does not keep them; extract, split and PNG export
    write through a temporary file, so a link at the target name is
    replaced, never followed; extracting onto the open document is refused;
    pages 0 wide or high no longer crash it; a huge page cannot fill the
    memory on PNG export; the file name in the status bar is plain text;
    saved files get the usual permissions (a replaced file keeps its own).
  * LinPaint+: images larger than 8000 x 8000 are refused when opened or
    pasted (the size is checked before decoding); the zoomed view and the
    undo steps are capped in memory (drawing stays where the mouse is);
    saving goes through a temporary file (a link at that name is replaced,
    never followed; permissions as usual); cancelling the
    screenshot dialog no longer opens a second one; helper commands only
    from the system folders.
  * vv-sandbox: PATH inside the sandbox is fixed to the system folders.
  * vv-sandbox: Aurora and VaeVictis PDF show the close button again (Qt's
    title bar on GNOME asked the desktop which buttons to draw, over a D-Bus
    connection the sandbox does not give; now it draws them itself).
  * VPN manager: the server list is readable (it showed light text on a
    white background).

 -- linuxtuts-website <292920559+linuxtuts-website@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200

vaevictis-tools (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release, for systems installed from VaeVictis OS 1.1. Fixes found
    with AI-assisted reviews (not an independent security audit):
  * vv-sandbox: only regular files given as arguments, after following links;
    a file reached through a link is bound alone and read-only; hidden files,
    files in hidden folders and in ~/bin are read-only; nothing of /run (except
    /run/media), /proc, /sys or /dev; never a folder; secrets moved elsewhere
    behind a link are hidden at their real place; the program gets the
    resolved paths of the accepted files only.
  * vv-vpn-helper: "Table" other than auto and "FwMark" are refused (custom
    routing could leave the traffic outside the tunnel while it shows as
    connected); every [Peer] needs an Endpoint; the kill switch flag is
    root-only; disconnecting a tunnel never releases the kill switch of
    another one.
  * VPN polkit rule: the password is asked at every action (auth_admin).
  * v-snap: refuses a system snapshot or restore when /usr, /usr/local, /var
    or /var/lib are separate partitions; never copies or deletes another
    snapshot store.
  * vv-vpn, vv-panic, vv-panic-action: commands only from the system folders.

 -- linuxtuts-website <292920559+linuxtuts-website@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0200
